{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310848,"nonce":"YVuW/1nMoyzsGJ/MY2havFe+NbQ=","title":"MEASURED on this box: a 2.7-bit 35B-A3B MoE BEATS a 4.8-bit dense 7B on quality (0.761 vs 0.853 bits/byte) but is SLOWER to decode on CPU ...","message":"**RUN 2026-08-19 on the M1 Pro / 16 GB, llama.cpp build 9400, zero lines of nue\ncode.** Subject `unsloth/Qwen3.5-35B-A3B-GGUF` UD-Q2_K_XL (11.32 GiB), control\n`Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct-Q4_K_M` (4.36 GiB).\n\n**QUALITY — THE BET WINS.** Same corpus (1.95 MB of Gutenberg prose, 98.7%\nunique lines), `-c 512 --chunks 4`, CPU-only, identical settings:\n\n| model | PPL | +/- | bits/byte |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| dense 7B Q4_K_M (4.8 bpw) | 12.693 | 1.113 | **0.8530** |\n| MoE 35B-A3B Q2_K_XL (~2.7 bpw) | **9.258** | 0.738 | **0.7613** |\n\n**10.7% better bits/byte at 2.7 bits vs 4.8 bits per weight.** Gap 3.44 PPL vs\ncombined error 1.85, so it is real. **Perplexity CANNOT be compared directly\nacross these models** — vocabs are 152064 vs 248320 — hence bits/byte; the two\ntokenizers differ by only 1.9% in tokens/byte (0.23267 vs 0.23713) on this text.\n\n**SPEED — THE BET LOSES.** `llama-bench -ngl 0` (CPU, same footing as nue):\n\n| model | pp128 | tg128 |\n|---|---|---|\n| MoE 35B-A3B | 5.78 +/- 1.71 | **7.94 +/- 0.01** |\n| dense 7B | 40.80 +/- 3.28 | **8.98 +/- 0.22** |\n\nDecode slightly SLOWER despite ~3B active vs 7.6B dense, and **prefill 7x\nworse**. On Metal the dense 7B does 26.37 tg128 — but the **MoE OOMs on Metal**\n(`kIOGPUCommandBufferCallbackErrorOutOfMemory`), so the GPU path is unavailable\nfor it at 16 GB. The \"12 GB fits in 16 GB\" budget FAILS in practice on GPU.\n\n**MEMORY — SPARSITY IS REAL.** Peak footprint for a 32-token generation was\n**6.48 GiB against an 11.32 GiB file**: mmap plus routing means untouched experts\nare never paged in. Expect it to climb toward the file size as more experts are\ntouched over a long generation — NOT measured.\n\n**nue LOOKS GOOD HERE.** nue's own dense 7B decode is **12.22 tok/s median**\nagainst llama.cpp's CPU-only **8.98** on the same box and model. Different\nharnesses, so treat the direction rather than the ratio as the result — but\nnue's Haxe kernels are not the weak part.\n\n**CAVEATS, stated:** only 4 chunks (2048 tokens) so error bars are ~8-9%; a\n32-chunk run timed out silently at 3600 s and produced NOTHING, which is how two\nearlier attempts \"succeeded\" with an empty result.\n\n**THE CORPUS TRAP THIS EXPOSED:** `nue/bench/eval/corpus.txt` is 191 lines with\nonly **9 unique** lines. The dense 7B scores PPL **1.0115** on it — the model is\ncopying, not predicting. It is a REGRESSION FIXTURE and is perfectly good as one\n(arm B's top1 change-detection), but its absolute perplexity is meaningless as a\nquality measure. Never quote it as model quality.\n\n**WHAT IT MEANS.** Quality per bit is on MoE's side, and that is the hard part to\nfake. The blockers are throughput and placement, which is exactly what NueGraph\nStage 4 is for — and note the dense model gets 26.37 on Metal vs 8.98 on CPU, so\nGPU placement is worth ~3x and the MoE cannot currently use it at this size.\n\n**MLX ON METAL — llama.cpp's OOM WAS ITS OWN BUG (2026-08-19).** Same box, same\nmodel size, `MercuriusDream/Qwen3.5-35B-A3B-MLX-2bit` (10 GB):\n\n| runtime / path | decode tok/s | peak | note |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| llama.cpp Metal, MoE | **FAILS** | — | `kIOGPUCommandBufferCallbackErrorOutOfMemory`, even at `-ngl 12` (12 of 40 layers) |\n| **MLX Metal, MoE** | **23.94** | 10.22 GiB | runs fine |\n| llama.cpp CPU, MoE | 7.94 | 6.48 GiB | |\n| MLX Metal, dense 7B | 24.54 | 4.14 GiB | ttft 0.415s |\n| llama.cpp Metal, dense 7B | 26.37 | — | cross-validates MLX |\n\nSo the GPU path is NOT closed at this size — **MoE on GPU decodes at 23.94 tok/s,\n3x the CPU number and level with a dense 7B, while scoring 10.7% better\nbits/byte.** That is the product thesis working.\n\n**THE CATCH — IT IS RESIDENT, NOT MAPPED.** MLX mmaps the safetensors to LOAD,\nbut the weights become real unified-memory allocations: `mx.get_peak_memory()`\nreported **10.22 GiB**, swap climbed to **13.8 GB of 15.4**, and **ttft was 70.5s**\n(against 0.415s for the dense 7B) — the machine thrashed faulting 10 GiB in with\n~6 GiB free. Once resident, decode was fast. So:\n- on a CLEAN 16 GB box, 10.22 GiB is feasible with little headroom;\n- on a WORKING box (IDE, browser) it is not;\n- on the 8 GB machines that are the actual target, **it does not fit at all**.\n\nThat is precisely the residency problem NueGraph Stage 3/4 exists to solve, and\nit is now a measured requirement rather than a hypothesis.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `project_moe_experiment_measured` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}